{"answer": "I could not find the exact rule, so this is partial. The evidence I found says a Lore skill's SKILL.md must contain a valid `## Help` block. The lore-help spec owns that Help-block convention, and CI enforces it through `scripts/check-skill-help.sh`. The knowledge graph also links lore-help to that script. A separate record says static skills seeded at lore-api boot were given a `## Help` block to satisfy requirement FR-010, and that they must be seeded with valid `## Help` blocks. I could not read `scripts/check-skill-help.sh` or `specs/lore-help/spec.md`. So I do not know what makes a Help block 'valid', such as required subheadings or content, or whether the PR check also tests the frontmatter.", "sources": ["lore memory episode session:re-cinq/lore (lore-help spec owns the Help-block convention and CI enforcement via scripts/check-skill-help.sh)", "lore memory episode session:re-cinq/lore (static skills seeded at boot receive a `## Help` block to satisfy FR-010)", "lore memory episode session:re-cinq/lore (static skills must be seeded with valid `## Help` blocks)", "lore knowledge graph: lore-help uses check-skill-help.sh", "lore memory session-summary/2026-10-13-org-skills-spec-plan"]}
